    <string name="setup_aap_title">Advanced Protection is on</string>
    <string name="setup_aap_text">It allows only assistive accessibility services, so BentoBar can\'t run. The Live Update chip and Quick Settings tiles still work.</string>
    <string name="setup_turn_on_title">Turn on BentoBar</string>
    <string name="setup_turn_on_text">Apps can draw on the status bar only through an accessibility service, which Android says can \"view and control your screen\". With it on, BentoBar:</string>
    <string name="setup_turn_on_draws">draws your items in the status bar\'s free space</string>
    <string name="setup_turn_on_reads">reads the status bar\'s layout, to find free space for your items</string>
    <string name="setup_turn_on_copies">reads the color of the status bar clock, so your items match it</string>
    <string name="setup_turn_on_runs">runs the system actions you pick in the Tools menu or as a Shortcut item, such as screenshot or lock</string>
    <string name="setup_turn_on_doesnt">It never reads what other apps show inside their windows, and doesn\'t watch your keyboard, mouse or touches. What it reads stays on your Googlebook: BentoBar never collects or shares it.</string>
    <string name="disclosure_title">BentoBar uses the Accessibility API</string>
    <string name="disclosure_intro">Android lets an app draw on the status bar only through an accessibility service (the AccessibilityService API). With its service on, BentoBar:</string>
    <string name="disclosure_next">Agree opens Android\'s Accessibility settings, where you turn BentoBar on. You can turn it off there at any time.</string>
    <string name="disclosure_agree">Agree</string>
    <string name="disclosure_decline">No thanks</string>
    <string name="setup_accessibility_settings">Accessibility settings</string>
    <string name="setup_turn_on">Turn on</string>
    <string name="setup_app_info">App info</string>
    <string name="setup_restricted_intro">Installed from a download? Android restricts the switch until you allow it:</string>
    <string name="setup_restricted_1">In Accessibility settings, open BentoBar and tap the switch. At \"Restricted setting\", tap OK.</string>
    <string name="setup_restricted_2">Tap App info above, then ⋮ › Allow restricted settings, and enter your PIN.</string>
    <string name="setup_restricted_3">Go back to Accessibility settings › BentoBar and turn it on.</string>
    <string name="setup_review_note">About a day later, Android asks you to review apps with full device access. That\'s routine for apps like this.</string>
    <string name="setup_notifications_title">Notifications</string>
    <string name="setup_notifications_text">For timer alerts and the Live Update chip.</string>
    <string name="setup_live_title">Live Updates</string>
    <string name="setup_live_text">Lets the Live Update chip appear in the status bar. On by default.</string>
    <string name="setup_open_setting">Open setting</string>
    <string name="setup_calendar_title">Calendar</string>
    <string name="setup_calendar_text">For the Next meeting item and events in the month view. Stays on this device.</string>
    <string name="setup_alarms_title">Alarms and reminders</string>
    <string name="setup_alarms_text">Lets timers ring on time while your Googlebook sleeps; without it, they may run late.</string>
    <string name="setup_usage_title">Usage access</string>
    <string name="setup_listener_title">Notification access</string>
    <string name="setup_listener_text">For Now playing: the title, artist and artwork. BentoBar tells Android to send it no notifications.</string>
    <string name="setup_online_title">Online services</string>
    <string name="setup_online_text">Three items go online, and only after you set them up: Weather asks Open-Meteo; Flight asks AirLabs and US stocks asks Finnhub, each with your own key. Everything else stays offline.</string>
    <string name="setup_online_weather">Weather (Open-Meteo)</string>
    <string name="setup_online_flights">Flights (AirLabs)</string>
    <string name="setup_online_weather_first">Search for a city, or choose My location, in a Weather item first</string>
    <string name="setup_online_flights_first">Save an AirLabs key in a Flight item first</string>
    <string name="setup_online_stocks">US stocks (Finnhub)</string>
    <string name="setup_online_stocks_first">Save a Finnhub key in a US stocks item first</string>
    <string name="setup_online_fx">Exchange rates (Frankfurter)</string>
    <string name="setup_online_fx_first">Turn on Exchange rates in an Exchange rates item first</string>
    <string name="setup_online_off_note">Off stops requests at once and deletes what was fetched.</string>
    <string name="about_version">BentoBar %1$s</string>
    <string name="about_tagline">Add, hide and organize items in your Googlebook\'s status bar.</string>
    <string name="about_formerly">Called DiscoBar until version 0.4.1.</string>
    <string name="about_privacy">Privacy</string>
    <string name="about_privacy_text">BentoBar goes online for three items only, and only after you set them up: Weather asks Open-Meteo about the city you search for (or, with My location, about where the device is, rounded to about 10 km), Flight asks AirLabs about the flight you enter, and US stocks asks Finnhub about the stocks you follow, the last two with a key of your own. BentoBar sends nothing else anywhere: not your layout, your calendar, what\'s playing or anything it measures. It has no accounts of its own, no ads and no analytics, and it sends its developer nothing. Its accessibility service reads the status bar (its layout and its clock\'s color) and, with the App menu bar on, which app is in front and the system\'s title bar over its window, never what apps show inside their windows. With Usage access on, it also reads how much data and storage each app uses, for the Network and Storage menus, and nothing else.</string>
    <string name="about_privacy_weather">Weather sends Open-Meteo the city you search for. For the forecast it sends the coordinates of the city you pick, rounded to about a kilometer: about every 30 minutes while the bar is on screen, and when you open its menu or press Refresh. With My location it sends where this device is instead, rounded to about 10 km: BentoBar asks Android for the approximate location only while such an item is in the bar or waits in Hidden to show for rain or snow, or while BentoBar\'s settings window is open, and keeps it and its forecast in memory only. Open-Meteo sees your IP address, as any website does, and may keep it in its logs for 90 days.</string>
    <string name="about_privacy_stocks">US stocks sends Finnhub your own key with the symbol of each stock you follow: every 2 minutes while the market is open and the bar is on screen, once after the close, and when you open its menu, press Refresh or save a new key. Its search sends what you type when you press Search. Finnhub sees your IP address and can tie requests to your key\'s account. BentoBar ships no key.</string>
    <string name="about_privacy_fx">Exchange rates asks Frankfurter (api.frankfurter.dev) for the European Central Bank\'s reference table of currencies at most once an hour while the bar is on screen, and when you open its menu or press Refresh. No key is needed, and the pairs you follow are calculated on your device. Frankfurter sees your IP address, as any website does.</string>
    <string name="about_privacy_flight">Flight sends AirLabs the flight number and your own key: when you track a flight, when you press Refresh or save a new key, and while it follows the flight, about every 3 hours until 3 hours before departure, then every 30 minutes or sooner until it lands; a failed try is repeated sooner. Once it knows the flight has landed it stops asking; after sleeping through a landing it asks once more. AirLabs sees your IP address and can tie lookups to your key\'s account. BentoBar ships no key. Open flight page opens FlightAware in your browser.</string>
    <string name="about_privacy_media">Now playing, with notification access, reads the title, artist and artwork your media players publish. BentoBar tells Android to send it no notifications, and receives none. Nothing is stored or sent.</string>
    <string name="about_privacy_others">Device batteries, Heat, World clock and the volume slider use only what Android tells every app. They need no permission and send nothing. New event hands the chosen time to your calendar app.</string>
    <string name="about_privacy_backup">If you use Android\'s backup, Android keeps a copy of BentoBar\'s settings in your Google account. Your AirLabs and Finnhub keys and the online switches are left out of it.</string>
    <string name="about_privacy_policy">Privacy policy</string>
    <string name="about_how">How it works</string>
    <string name="about_how_text">Apps can\'t change Android\'s own status bar icons, so BentoBar draws its items in its own windows over the status bar\'s free space. It moves them aside when the system icons change and hides them when an app goes full screen. A running timer or meeting can also appear as a Live Update chip.</string>
